+TRANSCRIPT: mat-practice-annotation-transcript is an unannotated practice stimulus: a fictional conversation of at least fourteen numbered lines (1., 2., ...) between an adviser and a participant who revise a prompt for fictional Juniper Garden Centre to send to fictional customer Northgate Dental Practice about quote PR-112 for EUR 128.50 for a plant-care visit on 21 June. Declare all these values fictional in an opening notice. Write it independently, never as a paraphrase or re-ordering of the approved assessment annotation transcript: do not start from a bare one-sentence request, do not follow its sequence of moves, and share no sequence of eight or more words with any approved assessment stimulus (the host rejects this). Begin from the participant's own partial draft that already contains some case details, then revise it in an order of your choosing. Quoted prompt text in some lines must actually supply a task directive, the specific case details and an output cue; other lines must only discuss adding a sample message or a reusable structure with slots, without supplying one, so learners can tell text given to the tool from talk about it. Never recap, list or summarise what the prompt contains or which moves were or were not supplied, and never say that something was only talked about. The notice and speech must not use the words instruction, primary content, example, cue, template, zero-shot, one-shot, few-shot, enacted or mentioned, or give any label, classification, hint or commentary that tells learners which move a line shows. Avoid absolute claims such as "relies entirely" or "always". No AI tool is run and no tool output is shown.
